\title{Aggregate Equivalence $\neq$ Behavioral Equivalence: Per-Example Multiplicity in Hybrid Linear Recurrent Models}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Hybrid linear recurrent architectures that appear interchangeable by aggregate benchmarks can differ on which specific examples they handle correctly. Using a controlled model zoo \citep{wang2025hybrid} (three architectures, five attention ratios, two scales), we decompose per-example effects of adding attention and find structured predictive multiplicity \citep{marx2020predictive}: pairwise overlap of helped and hurt example sets is uniformly low (Jaccard $\approx 0.10$, roughly twice the independence baseline; all $p < .01$ across five architectures). This multiplicity varies by task type or evaluation format ($\alpha = 0.14$--$0.30$) rather than attention ratio. Per-example overlap appears to stabilize at 4\% attention and remains flat across higher ratios, suggesting that minimal attention may establish which examples each architecture affects. \citet{yang2026benchmark} document analogous per-item disagreement (16--66\%) among frontier large language models (LLMs) despite comparable aggregate scores; our within-family design isolates backbone variation from training-regime differences. Confidence margins partially predict affected examples (area under the ROC curve [AUC] 0.70--0.74). Low cross-architecture overlap implies substantial complementarity, though routing-based exploitation remains task-dependent.
\end{abstract}

\input{sections/introduction}

\input{sections/related_work}

\input{sections/method}

\input{sections/experiments}

\input{sections/conclusion}

\section*{Limitations}

\paragraph{Single training runs.} Each configuration is a single training run; consistency across all 40 architecture--benchmark combinations (all $p < .01$) and a $3\times$ within- vs.\ cross-architecture Jaccard gap (within-seed $J$ is 2.7--4.3$\times$ higher; Appendix~\ref{app:seed_calibration}) mitigate seed confounds but cannot fully resolve them.

\paragraph{Format confound.} The confound between task type and evaluation format cannot be fully resolved: LAMBADA is the only free-form benchmark, so its higher $\alpha$ may reflect format differences rather than cognitive demands alone.

\paragraph{Coverage.} We examine three primary architectures (plus two extended) from one model zoo, four main benchmarks, and two scale points. Generalization to Mamba, RWKV, other hybrid families, and generation tasks remains open; all four per-example benchmarks use discriminative scoring, so multiplicity under open-ended generation is untested. Results at larger scales (e.g., 7B+) may differ, particularly as attention mechanisms become more expressive with increased capacity.

\paragraph{Pre-training only.} All evaluations use pretrained checkpoints without fine-tuning; per-example effects may not persist after task-specific adaptation. All evaluations use greedy decoding (the lm-eval-harness default); sampling strategies may alter per-example agreement patterns.

\bibliography{references}

\appendix
\input{sections/appendix}

\end{document}

\vspace{-2pt}
\section{Conclusion}

We have shown that hybrid linear recurrent architectures which appear interchangeable by aggregate benchmarks diverge substantially at the per-example level. This multiplicity varies by task type ($\alpha = 0.14$--$0.30$) rather than attention ratio. Per-example overlap appears to stabilize at 4\% attention (pairwise Jaccard $\leq 0.10$); two interpretations---architecture-specific fingerprints or rapid convergence toward Transformer-like behavior---remain open (Appendix~\ref{app:transformer_entrainment}). Confidence margins partially predict which examples are affected (AUC 0.70--0.74), with rescue thresholds architecture-invariant but disruption thresholds varying 47\%.

At the 340M--1.3B scales studied here, per-example audit before architecture substitution is advisable when multiplicity is substantial---aggregate equivalence does not guarantee behavioral equivalence. Low cross-architecture Jaccard creates complementarity ($+7$--$14$\,pp oracle gain), though preliminary routing experiments show exploitation remains task-dependent ($+5.3$\,pp on LAMBADA; $-0.6$\,pp on PIQA).

